# Helpers and scalar input controls

Say whether a field edits a household helper or commands equipment before you choose its control and feedback. A helper’s saved value can later drive an automation, so a “local helper” can still have consequences.

The examples on this page are simulated. They do not call Home Assistant.

<h2 id="values">Value families and their meaning</h2>

| Native value or helper | What to keep |
| --- | --- |
| Number or input number | The minimum, maximum, step, unit and supported slider or box. Say whether saving changes a device setting or a helper. |
| Select or input select | Only the options supplied. Keep the stored option exactly, including its case and any translated or custom label. |
| Text or input text | The length limit and the text or password mode. Keep a visible label, and never show a password in a summary or status message. |
| Date, datetime, time or input datetime | Which parts exist (date, time or both), the time zone meaning and validity. Do not add a time to a date-only value. |
| Switch or input boolean | The setting or power change it controls. Do not label every boolean “On” without its subject. |
| Counter | The current value, the step and the bounds, with separate increment, decrement and reset actions. Reset returns the counter to its configured initial value, which is not always zero. |
| Timer or schedule | The native active, paused and idle states, or the schedule intervals. Use [Schedules, calendars and timers](schedules-and-time.html). |

A group summarises its members. It is not automatically a new physical device. A scene applies stored settings, but that does not prove every member now matches. A script runs a sequence, and its running state does not confirm each physical action. An enabled automation controls future runs and does not show that its conditions are true. Keep these differences when a helper appears in an Entities row, a Tile feature or More info.

