<h2 id="operations">Buttons, remote commands and sirens</h2>

Treat a button or input button as a single operation. A button’s state is the time it was last pressed, so a last-pressed time records an activation and is not a lasting “on”. Use a verb that names the real operation. When you know what a counter reset targets, show it. Buttons that look the same can have very different effects, so do not infer behaviour from an icon.

Remote, infrared and radio-frequency entities can offer commands, and the feedback depends on the integration. Show only the capabilities the entity supplies. A sent command does not show that a receiver acted. Do not invent power, volume, pairing or learning controls because the domain exists. Sounding a siren can affect people nearby, so keep the real target, the tone and duration it supports, and any confirmation visible. Native permissions stay in charge.

