<h2 id="editing">Draft, saved value and observed result</h2>

Keep three values apart: the draft being edited, the value Home Assistant accepted, and a device’s later report where a device is involved. Do not overwrite a draft when an update arrives. Show the conflict and let the person decide. Check input against the current schema before sending, and keep what the person typed after a rejection. If a selected option disappears, say the value is unresolved and do not select the first replacement.

Show pending feedback on the control that is affected, not on the whole page. Block duplicate requests while the outcome is pending. A network timeout leaves the result unconfirmed, so do not resend automatically. The documentation forms below check only whether the draft is valid, and they say so.

<h2 id="accessibility">Accessible scalar controls</h2>

Use a labelled text input, select, checkbox, radios, number field or native range to match the value. A read-only number is text, not a disabled slider. Keep the current value and the draft distinguishable. Increase and decrease buttons need their target and step in the name. Do not capture arrow keys outside the focused control, and do not change a value when the person scrolls the page with the wheel.

Locale formatting must not change the stored value. Native date and time inputs need a clear time zone when they mean an instant. A colour choice needs a named alternative and an accessible value, because a swatch alone does not identify a mode or state.

<h2 id="acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

Test decimal and negative bounds, a counter reset when the counter is not zero, a missing choice, a hidden password, a date-only value and the repeated hour when the clocks go back. Try a repeated request and an outside update while editing. Check that opening a helper or editing an unsaved draft sends nothing. Creating a helper or saving a value can change native state and trigger dependent automations, so keep those consequences and explain them. Confirm that unsupported remote and siren actions are absent and that an accepted command is not shown as receiver confirmation.

For the native definitions, see the Home Assistant documentation for [Number](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/number/), [Select](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/select/), [Input text](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/input_text/), [Input datetime](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/input_datetime/), [Counter](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/counter/), [Button](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/button/) and [Remote](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/remote/).

<h2 id="evidence">Implementation and evidence boundary</h2>

This is a design reference, not a replacement for native Home Assistant components. The examples use invented records and local feedback. Mapping an entry here names a design owner. It does not show that every integration, browser or device has been tested. Keep native state formatting, translations, permissions, capabilities and event handling. Theme tokens change presentation, not behaviour.

The [interface coverage inventory](../reference/ha-interface-coverage.json) records the source baseline and every mapping: Home Assistant Core 2026.11.1 and frontend 20261028.5. Check native behaviour again after a frontend change, and record what was actually tested using [Native task accessibility and evidence](native-task-accessibility.html). Keep native controls at 16px text with visible labels, keyboard access and readable names at 320px, and do not remove a capability explanation or recovery route to make a control compact.
